=encoding utf8

=head1 NAME

SDL3::API::Video - functions and constants declared in SDL_video.h

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  use SDL3::API::Video ':all';

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Low-level binding generated from the C header. Functions keep their C
names and argument order. Pointers to structs are objects, out parameters
take scalar references, C<void *> becomes a L<SDL3::Pointer>.

=head1 FUNCTIONS

=head2 SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers

  my $result = SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers();

C: C<< int SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers(void) >>

Get the number of video drivers compiled into SDL.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

Returns integer: the number of built in video drivers.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetVideoDriver

  my $result = SDL_GetVideoDriver($index);

C: C<< const char * SDL_GetVideoDriver(int index) >>

Get the name of a built in video driver.

The video drivers are presented in the order in which they are normally checked during initialization.

The names of drivers are all simple, low-ASCII identifiers, like "cocoa", "x11" or "windows". These never have Unicode characters, and are not meant to be proper names.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item index - integer. the index of a video driver.

=back

Returns string: the name of the video driver with the given **index**, or NULL if index is out of bounds.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetCurrentVideoDriver

  my $result = SDL_GetCurrentVideoDriver();

C: C<< const char * SDL_GetCurrentVideoDriver(void) >>

Get the name of the currently initialized video driver.

The names of drivers are all simple, low-ASCII identifiers, like "cocoa", "x11" or "windows". These never have Unicode characters, and are not meant to be proper names.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

Returns string: the name of the current video driver or NULL if no driver has been initialized.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetSystemTheme

  my $result = SDL_GetSystemTheme();

C: C<< SDL_SystemTheme SDL_GetSystemTheme(void) >>

Get the current system theme.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

Returns integer: the current system theme, light, dark, or unknown.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetDisplays

  my $result = SDL_GetDisplays($count);

C: C<< SDL_DisplayID * SDL_GetDisplays(int * count) >>

Get a list of currently connected displays.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item count - reference to a scalar holding integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the number of displays returned, may be NULL.

=back

Returns SDL3::Pointer, object, string buffer or undef: a 0 terminated array of display instance IDs or NULL on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information. This should be freed with SDL_free() when it is no longer needed.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetPrimaryDisplay

  my $result = SDL_GetPrimaryDisplay();

C: C<< SDL_DisplayID SDL_GetPrimaryDisplay(void) >>

Return the primary display.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

Returns unsigned integer: the instance ID of the primary display on success or 0 on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetDisplayProperties

  my $result = SDL_GetDisplayProperties($displayID);

C: C<< SDL_PropertiesID SDL_GetDisplayProperties(SDL_DisplayID displayID) >>

Get the properties associated with a display.

The following read-only properties are provided by SDL:

- `SDL_PROP_DISPLAY_HDR_ENABLED_BOOLEAN`: true if the display has HDR headroom above the SDR white point. This is for informational and diagnostic purposes only, as not all platforms provide this information at the display level.

On KMS/DRM:

- `SDL_PROP_DISPLAY_KMSDRM_PANEL_ORIENTATION_NUMBER`: the "panel orientation" property for the display in degrees of clockwise rotation. Note that this is provided only as a hint, and the application is responsible for any coordinate transformations needed to conform to the requested display orientation.

On Wayland:

- `SDL_PROP_DISPLAY_WAYLAND_WL_OUTPUT_POINTER`: the wl_output associated with the display

On Windows:

- `SDL_PROP_DISPLAY_WINDOWS_HMONITOR_POINTER`: the monitor handle (HMONITOR) associated with the display

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=back

Returns unsigned integer: a valid property ID on success or 0 on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetDisplayName

  my $result = SDL_GetDisplayName($displayID);

C: C<< const char * SDL_GetDisplayName(SDL_DisplayID displayID) >>

Get the name of a display in UTF-8 encoding.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=back

Returns string: the name of a display or NULL on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetDisplayBounds

  my $result = SDL_GetDisplayBounds($displayID, $rect);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_GetDisplayBounds(SDL_DisplayID displayID, SDL_Rect * rect) >>

Get the desktop area represented by a display.

The primary display is often located at (0,0), but may be placed at a different location depending on monitor layout.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=item rect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure filled in with the display bounds.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetDisplayUsableBounds

  my $result = SDL_GetDisplayUsableBounds($displayID, $rect);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_GetDisplayUsableBounds(SDL_DisplayID displayID, SDL_Rect * rect) >>

Get the usable desktop area represented by a display, in screen coordinates.

This is the same area as SDL_GetDisplayBounds() reports, but with portions reserved by the system removed. For example, on Apple's macOS, this subtracts the area occupied by the menu bar and dock.

Setting a window to be fullscreen generally bypasses these unusable areas, so these are good guidelines for the maximum space available to a non-fullscreen window.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=item rect - SDL3::Rect object or undef. the SDL_Rect structure filled in with the display bounds.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetNaturalDisplayOrientation

  my $result = SDL_GetNaturalDisplayOrientation($displayID);

C: C<< SDL_DisplayOrientation SDL_GetNaturalDisplayOrientation(SDL_DisplayID displayID) >>

Get the orientation of a display when it is unrotated.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=back

Returns integer: the SDL_DisplayOrientation enum value of the display, or `SDL_ORIENTATION_UNKNOWN` if it isn't available.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetCurrentDisplayOrientation

  my $result = SDL_GetCurrentDisplayOrientation($displayID);

C: C<< SDL_DisplayOrientation SDL_GetCurrentDisplayOrientation(SDL_DisplayID displayID) >>

Get the orientation of a display.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=back

Returns integer: the SDL_DisplayOrientation enum value of the display, or `SDL_ORIENTATION_UNKNOWN` if it isn't available.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetDisplayContentScale

  my $result = SDL_GetDisplayContentScale($displayID);

C: C<< float SDL_GetDisplayContentScale(SDL_DisplayID displayID) >>

Get the content scale of a display.

The content scale is the expected scale for content based on the DPI settings of the display. For example, a 4K display might have a 2.0 (200%) display scale, which means that the user expects UI elements to be twice as big on this display, to aid in readability.

After window creation, SDL_GetWindowDisplayScale() should be used to query the content scale factor for individual windows instead of querying the display for a window and calling this function, as the per-window content scale factor may differ from the base value of the display it is on, particularly on high-DPI and/or multi-monitor desktop configurations.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=back

Returns number: the content scale of the display, or 0.0f on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetFullscreenDisplayModes

  my $result = SDL_GetFullscreenDisplayModes($displayID, $count);

C: C<< SDL_DisplayMode ** SDL_GetFullscreenDisplayModes(SDL_DisplayID displayID, int * count) >>

Get a list of fullscreen display modes available on a display.

The display modes are sorted in this priority:

- w -> largest to smallest - h -> largest to smallest - bits per pixel -> more colors to fewer colors - packed pixel layout -> largest to smallest - refresh rate -> highest to lowest - pixel density -> lowest to highest

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=item count - reference to a scalar holding integer, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled in with the number of display modes returned, may be NULL.

=back

Returns SDL3::Pointer, object, string buffer or undef: a NULL terminated array of display mode pointers or NULL on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information. This is a single allocation that should be freed with SDL_free() when it is no longer needed.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetClosestFullscreenDisplayMode

  my $result = SDL_GetClosestFullscreenDisplayMode($displayID, $w, $h, $refresh_rate, $include_high_density_modes, $closest);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_GetClosestFullscreenDisplayMode(SDL_DisplayID displayID, int w, int h, float refresh_rate, _Bool include_high_density_modes, SDL_DisplayMode * closest) >>

Get the closest match to the requested display mode.

The available display modes are scanned and `closest` is filled in with the closest mode matching the requested mode and returned. The mode format and refresh rate default to the desktop mode if they are set to 0. The modes are scanned with size being first priority, format being second priority, and finally checking the refresh rate. If all the available modes are too small, then false is returned.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=item w - integer. the width in pixels of the desired display mode.

=item h - integer. the height in pixels of the desired display mode.

=item refresh_rate - number. the refresh rate of the desired display mode, or 0.0f for the desktop refresh rate.

=item include_high_density_modes - boolean. boolean to include high density modes in the search.

=item closest - SDL3::DisplayMode object or undef. a pointer filled in with the closest display mode equal to or larger than the desired mode.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetDesktopDisplayMode

  my $result = SDL_GetDesktopDisplayMode($displayID);

C: C<< const SDL_DisplayMode * SDL_GetDesktopDisplayMode(SDL_DisplayID displayID) >>

Get information about the desktop's display mode.

There's a difference between this function and SDL_GetCurrentDisplayMode() when SDL runs fullscreen and has changed the resolution. In that case this function will return the previous native display mode, and not the current display mode.

This function should only be called on the main thread.

=over

=item displayID - unsigned integer. the instance ID of the display to query.

=back

Returns SDL3::DisplayMode object or undef: a pointer to the desktop display mode or NULL on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

